  "worked_solution": "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{\u2032}(0),$ we substitute $x=0$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(0) & =-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}+{0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-(0+0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
